# Playground demos

For adding or changing a live playground demo (`<HooksPlayground>`, the homepage's `Demo/code`). How playgrounds work (props, layouts, fixtures, design-system classes) is in the [Playground README](../../website/src/components/Playground/README.md).

## Goal

A demo soft-sells Data Client first and teaches users and agents how to use it second. People copy what they see and agents copy the code, so both the preview and the code teach.

- **Use case**: pick one that clearly shows the feature's advantage over the alternative (hand-rolled state, refetching, another library). If the preview doesn't make the advantage visible, pick another use case.
- **Mechanism**: make it easy to see how it works and why it's better: the reader can point at what the feature did (the row that updated in place, one request instead of three, the entry in the Store).
- **Look**: very beautiful, built from the playground design system so it reads as a real app. Styling goes in the design system, never inline styles or CSS in the example code, so a look changes in one place.
- **Scope**: as simple as possible while still showing maximum value, utility and related features. Cut whatever doesn't serve the point; add a related feature only when it shows more of the value.
- **New ideas**: coming up with a new demo idea needs at least high reasoning effort, since the idea decides everything above. Below that, ask the user to raise it (or hand the ideation to a subagent at high) before choosing.

## Conventions from review

- The demo makes its point by itself: no captions, narration or "watch this" text in the preview. Needing one means the use case doesn't show the advantage yet.
- Loading states are skeletons (`<Loading />`, `skeleton`), never "Loading…" text: the demo shows what a good app does.
- A demo of a real public API keeps the real URL and response shape in its code and is intercepted by a fixture in `website/src/fixtures/`, so copied code returns real data. Live calls from the playground hit rate limits.
- Code models what a careful app would do (`React.memo` on a list whose parent re-renders per keystroke, stable keys) even when the perf win is tiny, because readers copy it.
- Use `row` (code beside the preview) when the code is the lesson, even for routed or `autoRun` demos. Use `demo` (code behind a toggle) only when the point is purely visual, and ask the user first.
- Anything extra a demo needs (a clock, toasts) goes in the demo code using a design-system component or class, never a new Playground prop: the Playground is shared by every demo.
- Check it at phone width and in both light and dark themes: readers see all of them, and the site must work on every one.
